faqs:
  - question: Is fibromyalgia a real condition?
    answer: >-
      Yes. Fibromyalgia is a recognised long-term condition. It does not show
      up on X-rays or blood tests, but research shows that the nervous system
      of people with fibromyalgia processes pain differently. Your pain is
      real.
  - question: Is fibromyalgia a disability?
    answer: >-
      It can be. Under the Equality Act 2010 you are disabled if a physical or
      mental condition has a substantial, long-term effect on your ability to
      do normal daily activities. Long-term means it has lasted, or is likely
      to last, at least 12 months. Many people with fibromyalgia meet this
      definition, which gives them legal protection at work and in other
      places.
  - question: Can I get PIP for fibromyalgia?
    answer: >-
      PIP is not based on your diagnosis. It is based on how your condition
      affects your daily life. Many people with fibromyalgia do get PIP,
      because pain, tiredness and brain fog can make everyday tasks hard to do
      safely, well and as often as needed. Our free PIP points self-check can
      help you see which activities may apply to you.
  - question: Does fibromyalgia get worse over time?
    answer: >-
      Fibromyalgia does not damage your joints, muscles or organs. Symptoms
      often go up and down, with bad spells called flares. For many people,
      symptoms get easier to manage once they find the right mix of
      treatment, pacing and support.
---

Fibromyalgia (you say it "fy-bro-my-al-ja") is a condition that causes **long-lasting pain all over the body**. Most people also have **extreme tiredness** (fatigue), **sleep that does not refresh them**, and trouble with **memory and concentration**, often called "fibro fog".

It is a **long-term** condition. There is no cure yet, but there are treatments and ways of managing it that can make a real difference to daily life.

{% callout type="good" title="You are not alone" %}
Fibromyalgia is common. The NHS says it may affect as many as 1 in 20 people in the UK. It is also one of the most common conditions among people who get Personal Independence Payment (PIP).
{% /callout %}

## Common symptoms

Symptoms vary from person to person, and they can change from day to day. The main ones are:

- **widespread pain**, which may feel like an ache, a burning feeling or a sharp, stabbing pain
- **being very sensitive to pain**, so that a light touch or a small knock hurts much more than it should
- **extreme tiredness** that does not go away with rest
- **stiffness**, especially first thing in the morning
- **poor sleep**, waking up feeling as if you have not slept
- **fibro fog**, which means problems with memory, focus and finding the right words
- **headaches** and migraines
- **irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)**, with tummy pain and bloating

Many people also feel low or anxious. That is not surprising when you live with pain every day, and it is not a sign that the pain is "all in your head".

Read more about [fibromyalgia symptoms and flares](/conditions/physical/fibromyalgia/symptoms/).

## What causes fibromyalgia?

The exact cause is not yet known. Experts think it is linked to changes in the way the brain and nerves **process pain signals**, so that the body's "volume control" for pain is turned up too high.

It often starts after a stressful event, such as:

- an injury or an operation
- an infection or illness
- giving birth
- a very stressful time, such as a bereavement or the end of a relationship

It can also run in families. Sometimes there is no clear trigger at all.

## Who gets fibromyalgia?

Anyone can get fibromyalgia, including children and teenagers. It is **more common in women** than in men. It usually starts between the ages of 25 and 55.

People who have other long-term conditions, such as rheumatoid arthritis, lupus or osteoarthritis, are more likely to have it too.

## How it is diagnosed

There is no single test for fibromyalgia. A GP or a specialist will usually:

- ask about your symptoms and how long you have had them
- examine you
- arrange blood tests to rule out other conditions with similar symptoms

It can take a long time to get a diagnosis. Keeping a simple diary of your symptoms can help. Read our guide on [getting diagnosed with fibromyalgia](/conditions/physical/fibromyalgia/diagnosis/).

## Treatment and self-care

Treatment aims to ease your symptoms and help you do more of the things that matter to you. It usually works best as a **mix of approaches**, such as:

- **gentle, regular exercise**, built up slowly
- **talking therapies**, which can help you cope with pain and its effects
- **some medicines**, such as certain antidepressants, which can help with pain and sleep
- **pacing**, which means planning your activity so you avoid the "boom and bust" cycle

See our guide to [fibromyalgia treatment](/conditions/physical/fibromyalgia/treatment/) and our tips for [living well with fibromyalgia](/conditions/physical/fibromyalgia/living-with-fibromyalgia/).

## Fibromyalgia and benefits

If fibromyalgia makes everyday tasks hard, you may be able to get **Personal Independence Payment (PIP)** if you are under State Pension age, or **Attendance Allowance** if you are over it. These benefits are not means-tested, so your income and savings do not matter, and you can get them whether or not you work.

What counts is how your condition affects you, especially on bad days, not the name of the condition. Read our guide to [fibromyalgia and benefits](/conditions/physical/fibromyalgia/benefits/).

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## When to see a GP

See a GP if you have pain in many parts of your body that has lasted for 3 months or more, especially if you also have extreme tiredness or poor sleep.

{% callout type="danger" title="Get help urgently if" %}
You have new pain along with a high temperature, unexplained weight loss, weakness in your arms or legs, or problems controlling your bladder or bowels. Call NHS 111 or your GP straight away. Call 999 if it is an emergency.
{% /callout %}
